# A generated `hash` read as a function value (`row.hash.copy`) is a function
# like any other: its `_ft` typedef is written with it. Its definition takes
# the value by pointer, and the typedef says so. The type had no typedef: the
# pass that writes them reads declarations, and a generated member has none.
# A read of `r.hash` itself is the type's hash of the value and needs no
# function at all.
row: record {
    a: i64
    b: i64
}
main: function is {
    h: row.hash.copy
    r: row a: 1 b: 2
    print "\{(h this: r) == r.hash}"
}
